  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2)/2, drawn as a surface at 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first; above a learning rate of 0.8 (2/2.5) swings along x grow.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per component has sd 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b, like sampling b of 64 points; it is exactly 0 at 64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at true length in bowl units (scale 1): orange is the mini-batch step actually taken next; grey is −learning rate × the true slope. Step length readout is the orange arrow's length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when loss exceeds 6, the loss at the bowl's drawn corner (diverged). One update every 5 host ticks (6 per second at speed 1).",
    "Trails hold up to 41 points: the start plus one per step. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence; a new run uses the next noise values.",
    "Very large losses are capped on screen; the Loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}